red polling showing Baumgartner ahead 53-47"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point clearly to Baumgartner: an R+5 district, incumbency, and a dominant money advantage, while raters mostly place the race at Likely R or better. The only general-election polls, though partisan and months old, also show him ahead, so the forecast sits slightly beyond the baseline at an eight-point Republican margin.", "watch": "A late surge in Democratic outside spending or a polling shift toward Conroy could pull the race back toward the Lean R range.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Michael Baumgartner (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Michael Baumgartner seeks a second term in Washington's 5th District against Democrat Carmela Conroy in a rematch. The district leans Republican by about five points, and Baumgartner holds a substantial financial advantage, having raised roughly four times as much as Conroy and entering the fall with far more cash on hand. Outside groups have spent modestly against him, but little on his behalf.\n\nExpert raters broadly favor the incumbent, with several calling the race Safe R and the consensus at Likely R. The limited polling available, conducted in the spring with Democratic sponsorship, showed Baumgartner ahead by about six points. Markets price his chances near 80 percent. The forecast expects a Republican win by roughly eight points, with meaningful uncertainty given the absence of recent high-quality polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0968, "margin": -8.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1087, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0951, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0945, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1116, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0732, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0882, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2